  "notice": ["τίς ἐστιν; — θεός ἐστιν.", "τί ἐστιν; — ἵππος ἐστίν.", "ὁ ἵππος οὐκ ἄνθρωπός ἐστιν.", "ὁ θεός · ἡ θεά · τὸ ἄνθος"],
  "grammar": {
    "md": "Nothing to memorize yet: this lesson is for the ear and the eye. Notice three things and Unit 1 will make them explicit.\n\n1. **Question and answer.** τίς ἐστιν; 'who is it?' and τί ἐστιν; 'what is it?' are answered with a noun and ἐστίν: θεός ἐστιν, ἵππος ἐστίν. There is no word for 'a'.\n2. **Three little words for 'the'.** ὁ before some nouns (ὁ θεός, ὁ ἵππος), ἡ before others (ἡ θεά, ἡ γῆ), τό before others again (τὸ ἄνθος, τὸ πῦρ). They are the three genders. Learn each noun with its word for 'the'.\n3. **Not.** οὐ before a consonant, οὐκ before a vowel, οὐχ before a vowel with an *h*: οὐ θεά, οὐκ ἄνθρωπος, οὐχ ἵππος.\n\n**Numbers.** The Greeks wrote numbers with letters and a tick: α´ β´ γ´ δ´ ε´ ϛ´ ζ´ η´ θ´ ι´ (ϛ, *stigma*, is an old letter kept for 6). Say the words; the letters will come back in the readings.",
    "paradigms": ["article", "eimi"],
    "diagram": null
  },
